    <h2 id="success">Student success</h2>

    <p class="lede">Students I have taught, coached, or mentored have gone on to a position in a United States Navy fleet band, to the Chautauqua summer instrumental program, and to a touring position with Tim Zimmerman and the King&rsquo;s Brass.</p>

    <p>Others have gone on to undergraduate and graduate study at the Eastman School of Music, Rice University, Carnegie Mellon University, and DePaul University. An earlier student received a merit-based music scholarship to Harvard University.</p>

    <h2>Teaching philosophy</h2>

    <p>I teach students to hear a musical idea first and then find the physical action that produces it. Problems that present as technical &mdash; a high register that thins out, an articulation that will not speak &mdash; are usually problems of concept before they are problems of equipment or effort. Naming the sound we are after gives a student something specific to aim at, and gives both of us a way to tell whether the change actually worked.</p>

    <p>I want students to understand why a choice works rather than to copy mine. A student who can reproduce a teacher&rsquo;s phrasing has borrowed an interpretation; a student who can explain why the phrase moves where it does can build the next one alone. Lessons are structured so that students diagnose their own playing out loud, and so that they leave with a practice plan rather than a list of corrections.</p>

    <p>Work on vocal repertoire shapes how I teach line and articulation. Transcribing Vaughan Williams for trombone meant starting from text &mdash; where consonants place, how diction shapes attack and release, where a singer takes time and why. Those questions transfer directly to brass playing, and they give students a vocabulary for phrasing that is more precise than being told to be more musical.</p>

    <p>Students arrive preparing for different careers, and I treat all of them as legitimate. Orchestral auditions, graduate study, military bands, teaching, chamber music, and portfolio careers each demand different preparation. A studio that prepares only for one of them is preparing most of its students for something they are not going to do.</p>

    <h2>In the studio</h2>

    <p>At the University of Minnesota I am primary teacher of record for undergraduate and graduate applied trombone and euphonium. I direct and coach the trombone choir, run studio class, build curriculum and syllabi, serve on juries and recital committees, and lead outreach performances. For three years I was the sole instructor of record for Brass Methods, teaching trumpet, horn, trombone, euphonium, and tuba to future music educators.</p>

    <p>Before and alongside that work I have taught in the Academy of Music at the University of Northwestern, the Greater Twin Cities Youth Symphonies, the Mannes Preparatory Division, Boston College High School, and at Chautauqua &mdash; elementary through adult, majors and non-majors, in private lessons, sectionals, masterclasses, chamber coaching, and audition preparation.</p>
